  "enhanced_information": "The 65-year-old man presents with a vesicular rash around the eye, which is highly suggestive of herpes zoster ophthalmicus, a condition caused by the reactivation of the varicella-zoster virus. The burning and itching sensations, along with the vesicular rash, align with the typical presentation of this viral infection. Acyclovir is the first-line antiviral treatment for herpes zoster, particularly in older adults, as it can reduce the severity and duration of the symptoms and prevent complications such as postherpetic neuralgia. Other options, such as oral steroids, dietary changes, topical mupirocin, or topical steroids, are not appropriate first-line treatments for this condition. Therefore, the best next step in management is to initiate treatment with Acyclovir."
